Safe vs. mailbox analogy

A symmetric algorithm is similar to a safe to which a combination is given. It can be opened with any of the combinations and put things in/out.
Public-key cryptography was invented by Diffie and Hellman in 1976. The NSA earlier said they knew of the leak but did not provide evidence.
It is based on two keys; calculating the private key from the public key is difficult.
The public-key cryptography is like having a mail box. Anybody can put mail in (encrypt with public key), but only the key holder can retrieve it (decrypt it with private key).

Mathematical basis

It is based on trap door one way functions. The easy way is to encrypt, the encryption instructions are the public key.
Without the secret (private key) it is not easy to decrypt. Using the secret, it is as easy to decrypt as it is to encrypt.

Alice sends a message to Bob.Alice sends a message to Bob (Basic protocol).

Alice and Bob agree to a public key cryptosystem.
Bob shares his Public key with Alice.
Alice uses Bob's public key to encrypt her message and sends it to Bob.
Bob decrypts with his private key.
This alleviates the key-management issue on symmetric systems. No pre-agreement made and there is no need for a courier.
Eve has the public key and the ciphertext, but is unable to obtain the message or the private key.

Network version

All users have a key pair, while all public keys are shared in a database.
Alice looks up Bob's public key, encrypts, and sends the message. Bob is only required to act when he decides to read it just like in the case of ordinary mail.

Hybrid cryptosystems

In practice public-key algorithms are used to encrypt keys, rather than messages, because:
Speed: The symmetric algorithms are typically at least 1000 times quicker. Bandwidth requirements continue to rise and this will have significance for many years.
Chosen-plaintext vulnerability: the encrypting key is known to the attacker, so he can encrypt all the possible plain texts and match them with the cipher text. This is useful if the collection of messages that can be sent is limited, like a dollar amount less than $1,000,000.
This is not something that is possible with symmetric ciphers as the attacker does not have access to the key to do trial encryptions. It can also be solved by probabilistic encryption (Section 23.15).

Hybrid protocol:
Bob gives Alice his public key.
Alice chooses a random session key K, encrypts it with Bob's public key and sends it (E_B(K)).
Bob is able to decrypt it using his private key to retrieve K.
They both use a symmetric algorithm, K, for all subsequent traffic.
The session key is only available during use and is destroyed after use, thus minimising the chance of the session key being compromised.
The private key is also used only once per communication, which means that it is less risky.
