Merkle's puzzles

Ralph Merkle invented the first construction of public-key cryptography in 1974, as a Berkeley student. The idea his professor had wasn't understood, and he withdrew from the class.
The concept lies on "puzzles" which are easier for legitimate parties than for an eavesdropper:
Bob generates roughly 2²⁰ (one million) messages each of which has a puzzle number x and a secret key y. He encrypts each one with a different 20-bit key and sends all these to Alice.
Alice takes one out of the hat at random and does brute force on it – it's a large but manageable number of operations.
Alice uses the key she has recovered to encrypt her message and sends it to Bob together with x.
Bob is able to decrypt because he knows which key y corresponds to which puzzle x.
The puzzles have to be brute-forcely solved by Eve, which takes approximately 2⁴⁰ work. The X's don't help her either, as they are randomly assigned.
The benefit is approximately 'n compared to n²'. This is not a lot by cryptographic criteria, but there are situations where it will be adequate.
To see how long it would take Bob and Alice to do the same task with similar resources, the example suggests about one minute, while Eve with similar resources would be about a year.
