One-time trust setup:
Both parties create their own keypair using gpg --gen-key.
Both export their public key using gpg --export --armor --output <file> and this results in a printable ASCII encoding of the public key.
The other party imports with gpg --import.
They use a separate means of communication (e.g. phone call) to compare fingerprints (gpg --fingerprint).
They sign the verified key using gpg --sign-key, and then it has "full" trust.
Encrypting a message: gpg --encrypt --recipient <email> --sign --armor --output encrypted.pgp message.txt.
It is encrypted using Tero's public key so it can only be read by Tero.
It's encrypted with Alice's private key, which allows Tero to confirm that it's coming from Alice.
Decrypted.pgp is decrypted with the private key; gpg --decrypt encrypted.pgp. If the signature is good, it also returns "Good signature from Alice [full]"

Main lessons:
Public keys can be sent across an untrusted network, but need to be verified.
Private keys are kept confidential. If a key has been compromised due to leakage, it should be deleted from all uses.
Encryption gives confidentiality. Signing is authentic and integrity.

