Refer to the course reading: This is a hybrid system (like in Schneier 2.5). The actual data is encrypted using symmetric encryption, while the keys are shared using public-key cryptography and used to authenticate users (signatures).

Role of each key type.

Public key: key of the server certificate to verify the signature of the server, ephemeral public key: public keys are fed into the key agreement.
Private key: the long term key of the server is used to sign the handshake. The ephemeral private keys are only used to agree on a key and then destroyed.
TLS 1.3 does not support RSA key transport, and requires forward security. Later leaking of the server's private key won't decrypt old traffic, since the session keys are generated from ephemeral keys. 
